// HashMap<K, V> -- an associative container, in Prismio.
//
// REQUIREMENTS 13 asked for `HashMap<K,V>` and a growable `Vec<T>`. Only one of
// those was missing: `Vec<T>` **is** the growable vector -- runtime/lang_runtime.c
// stores it as a `void**` that doubles on push, and its own comment calls
// `list_new_with_capacity` "Vec::with_capacity". This is the half that did not
// exist.
//
// **A hash table since 2026-08-29, and the previous version of this header is
// worth keeping in view because it named its own fix.** It read:
//
// > Association list, not a hash table, and the reason is a missing language
// > feature rather than a shortcut. Hashing `K` needs a hash function per key
// > type, and dispatching to it needs bounded type parameters -- traits. […]
// > Give it bounds and this file becomes a hash table without its callers
// > changing.
//
// That is exactly what happened. `K: Key + Copy` uses `Key` (std/key.psm) for
// `hash`/`eq` and `Copy` (std/copy.psm) for storage; `indexOf` went from a
// linear scan to a probe, and **no caller
// changed** -- `tests/test_65_map.psm` is untouched and still passes.
//
// **String keys work now.** The old header said they "do not instantiate, and
// that is the language being consistent rather than this module being weak":
// `String ==` compares pointers and is rejected, so a generic `K` could only be
// compared with the operator, so only scalars instantiated. `Key` replaces the
// operator with `eq`, and `impl Key for String` uses `strEquals`. Struct keys
// work too, for any struct that implements `Key`.
//
// **The representation**: two dense parallel arrays in insertion order, plus an
// open-addressed `slots` table of indices into them. Insertion order is not an
// accident to be preserved -- `keyAt`/`valueAt` are the iteration API and
// their contract says insertion order, so the entries have to live somewhere
// that is not the probe table.
//
// **Keys and values may both be owned.** `K: Key + Copy` and `V: Copy` duplicate a
// `String`, a `Vec` or a struct that implements `Copy` into the table, and the table
// releases them: `HashMap<String, String>` and `HashMap<Int, Vec<Int>>` read
// 0 leaked, 0 violations under `--verify` (tests/test_map_owned.psm).
//
// **The rule that makes it hold: a stored value never leaves the table, only copies
// of it do.** `get`, `getOr`, `m[k]`, `take`, `set`'s answer, `getOrInsert*`,
// `valueAt` and `values()` all return `copyOf(list_get(...))`, as `Vec.get<T: Copy>`
// does, and `getOr` copies its `fallback` too (returning the borrowed parameter was a
// leak and a failed `--verify` fact). Until 2026-11-12 this file said the opposite:
// bounding `V: Copy` had been tried on 2026-08-29 and leaked every value, because the
// readers returned `list_get(m.entryValues, at)` itself, the analysis saw the values
// list escape through the return, and it declined to release the container. Returning
// copies is what removes the escape; the ownership rewrite since then (producers own
// their result, `param_returns`) is what lets the copies be released at the call site.
// For a scalar `copyOf` is the identity and costs nothing: the update and removal
// loops time the same as before the bound.
//
// **What it costs.** A read of an owned value allocates a copy, so a mutation in place
// (`m[k].push(x)`) is not expressible: read, change the copy, store it back.
//
// **What it buys, and what proves it.** A value displaced by `set` and a key or value
// removed by `remove`, `take`, `retain` or `clear` is released at the store, not parked
// until the map dies. The compiler releases a displaced element at once only when it can
// show no view of an element is live. It could for a fresh local `Vec`; for a field it
// proves it per program (src/ir/columns.psm): a private `Vec` field is a *closed column*
// when every use of it in the module is a list builtin, every `list_get` of it is
// consumed where it stands, and nothing a view is live across can store into it. This
// file is written to pass that proof and does not promise it: **an element is only ever
// viewed as an argument of the call consuming it (a probe's `eq`, a `hashSetProbe`), or
// copied with `copyOf` before anything else happens to it**, so `keyAt`, `valueAt`,
// `keys()`, `values()`, `for key in set`, and the closures `update` and `retain` take
// copies. A method that bound a view across a store, returned one, or pushed one into
// another container would not be wrong at run time -- it would leave its column parked,
// which `PRISMIO_COLUMN_TRACE=1` names at compile time, and `tests/test_map_owned.psm`'s
// churn holds to a peak. (Until 2026-11-14 the checker trusted these two columns by their
// name, and the proof found the promise broken: `values()` of a `HashMap<String, Vec<Int>>`
// pushed each stored `Vec` itself into its result -- 5 leaks and a failed `--verify` fact
// -- and `HashSet`'s `retain`, `insertAll`, `removeAll`, the set algebra and `for key in
// set` handed views to callees that may mutate the set.)
//
// **Triangular probing, with tombstones for removal.** `remove` cannot empty
// a bucket -- a later key's probe may pass through it -- so it marks the bucket
// with the tombstone value: a lookup probes past one, an insertion reuses the
// first it passes. Tombstones count against the load that triggers growth, and a
// removal that leaves more than a quarter of the table tombstoned rebuilds it in
// place, so an empty bucket always ends a probe.
//
// **Removal swap-removes the dense entry.** The last entry moves into the
// removed one's position (`list_swap`, then `list_truncate`, which releases the
// removed key), so removal is O(1) and nothing is left dead in `entryKeys`.
// The cost is order: positions are insertion order until the first removal,
// and after one the moved entry has the removed one's position -- the contract
// of IndexMap's `swap_remove`.
//
// Integer keys were never a consolation prize for the caller this was written
// for: src/aifcore/ interns every name, type and site to an `int` id
// and keys its tables on those.
//
// **That used to be the argument for an avalanche hash and it is the argument
// against one.** The header here read "consecutive small ids with an identity
// hash are one long probe chain", which is true of *linear* probing and false
// of this table: triangular offsets and a power-of-two mask place consecutive
// ids in consecutive buckets, one probe each, and measured they cost **1.00**
// probes per lookup where MurmurHash3's finalizer costs 1.21 — because 1.21 is
// what random placement costs and a dense key set does better than random.
// Scattering them also throws away the prefetching that makes a scan of an
// id-keyed table fast: 7.25 ms against 3.64 ms on `key_value_update`'s update
// phase. `keyMixInt` folds the high half into the low half and stops, and
// `mapRehash` below is what makes that safe.

import std.key
import std.copy
import std.default
import std.option

// `slots` holds an index into `keys`/`values`, or -1 for empty. Its length is
// always a power of two, so `hash & mask` is the bucket.
//
// **Two invariants this file keeps and promises to LLVM** (`__builtin_assume`, in
// mapProbe, `set` and `getOr`): `slots` has `mask + 1` elements, so a bucket is
// always inside it; and an entry index a bucket holds is below `entryKeys`'s and
// `entryValues`'s length, which are always equal. The bounds checks they make
// redundant cost key_value_update 13% and mixed_map_removal 10%: they grew mapProbe
// past what LLVM would inline. Every column is private, so nothing outside this file
// can break either one; a false promise would be undefined behaviour.
lang("hash_map") public struct HashMap<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy> {
    entryKeys: Vec<K>,
    entryValues: Vec<V>,
    slots: Vec<Int>,
    mask: Int,
    // Buckets a removal emptied (MAP_TOMBSTONE), which a lookup must probe past
    // and an insertion may reuse. Counted so the table can be rebuilt before
    // they crowd out the empty buckets that end a probe.
    tombstones: Int,
    // The most buckets past its home any entry sits, so a lookup can stop after
    // that many and a miss does not walk to an empty bucket. See mapProbe.
    longest: Int,
    // How far past its home the bucket the last `findSlot` probe answered is,
    // so mapInsert need not walk the sequence again to update `longest`. A
    // field rather than work inside mapProbe: anything more in the probe's body
    // stops it inlining into `set`'s overwrite path, and key_value_update went
    // from 10.7 to 14.8 ms when it did.
    placed: Int,
    // Whether this map has measured its own probe displacement and found the
    // hashes it is being given bad enough to be worth re-mixing. See mapRehash.
    strong: Bool
}

// 8 buckets, which is 4 entries before the first rehash. Small enough that a map
// used as a scratch lookup costs almost nothing, and a power of two so the mask
// is right from the start.
private fn mapInitialCapacity() -> Int { return 8 }

// A bucket's value when no entry was ever placed there, and when one was
// removed. Every other value is an index into `entryKeys`.
private fn mapEmpty() -> Int { return -1 }
private fn mapTombstone() -> Int { return -2 }

// An empty map.
lang("map_new") public fn mapNew<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>() -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let mut k: Vec<K> = list_new()
    let mut v: Vec<V> = list_new()
    let mut s: Vec<Int> = list_new_with_capacity(mapInitialCapacity())
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < mapInitialCapacity()) {
        list_push(s, -1)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return HashMap<K, V> { entryKeys: k, entryValues: v, slots: s, mask: mapInitialCapacity() - 1,
                       tombstones: 0, longest: 0, placed: 0, strong: false }
}

// The value under `key`, stored first as `V`'s own default when the key is absent:
// `groups.getOrInsertDefault(name)` for a `Vec` value starts the list the way
// `m.entry(k).or_default()` does. One lookup on a hit; the default is built on a miss
// only. A copy, as every reader answers: to add to the stored value, `modify` it.
public fn getOrInsertDefault<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy + Default>(m: HashMap<K, V>, key: K) -> V {
    return m.getOrInsertWith(key, || V.default())
}

// Room for `count` entries before the first rehash: the smallest power of two
// that keeps `count` at or under half full (mapInsert's growth rule), and never
// fewer buckets than mapNew's.
//
// **Grown from mapNew, not built beside it, and that is a leak fix.** A second
// function allocating a HashMap's columns gives AIF two containers that
// `m.entryKeys` may name, and a key mapInsert copies in is then judged able to
// land in either: tier T3, no element release, and every String key leaked
// (KNOWN_ISSUES, "A struct built by two constructors"). One construction site
// keeps one container per column.
public fn mapWithCapacity<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(count: Int) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapNew<K, V>()
    let mut buckets = m.mask + 1
    if (buckets / 2 >= count) { return m }
    while (buckets / 2 < count) { buckets = buckets * 2 }
    list_reserve(m.entryKeys, count)
    list_reserve(m.entryValues, count)
    list_reserve(m.slots, buckets)
    for i in (m.mask + 1)..<buckets { list_push(m.slots, -1) }
    m.mask = buckets - 1
    return m
}

// A map literal whose keys it does not own -- literals, names, keys of a type
// that owns nothing -- in one call: `{ "a": 1, "b": 2 }` reaches codegen as
// `mapFromEntries(2, ["a", "b"], [1, 2])` (src/sema/maps.psm). Sized once, so
// building it never rehashes.
//
// **A producer, and that is the point of it.** It builds the map it returns, so
// the literal's result is owned wherever it lands. A chain of `mapPut`s, which this replaced, returned the
// map it was given, and a pass-through's result stored in a struct field is
// never released (KNOWN_ISSUES, "A pass-through result in a struct field"), so a
// literal is built this way whenever it can be. The frame arrays hold nothing
// they would have to release, and a `[K]` parameter does not carry its length,
// so sema passes the count it wrote.
lang("map_from_entries") public fn mapFromEntries<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(n: Int, keys: [K], values: [V]) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(n)
    for i in 0..<n { m.set(keys[i], values[i]) }
    return m
}

// A map literal that holds a computed owned key or value -- `{ word(1): word(2) }` -- in one
// call, the way `vecOf` builds a Vec literal: each key and value is an argument, so the
// temporaries are released after the call, and the function builds the map it returns, so
// the result is owned wherever it lands (a `let`, a struct field, a `return`). One overload
// per count, because there are no varargs; src/sema/maps.psm picks `mapEntriesN` for
// 1..12 entries and merges runs of twelve (`mapMerge`) past that. A key written twice keeps
// its last value, as `set` would.
//
// **Not a chain of `mapPut`s** (a function that no longer exists). A wrapper that takes the map and hands it back is the
// pass-through shape whose result in a struct field or a `return` is never released
// (KNOWN_ISSUES), and it was measured: the chain leaked the whole table in a field.
private fn mapStore<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(m: HashMap<K, V>, key: K, value: V) {
    let found = mapProbe(m, key, true)
    if (found >= 0) {
        list_set(m.entryValues, found, copyOf(value))
        return
    }
    mapInsert(m, key, value, 0 - found - 2)
}

public fn mapEntries1<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(1)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries2<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(2)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries3<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V, k2: K, v2: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(3)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    mapStore(m, k2, v2)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries4<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V, k2: K, v2: V, k3: K, v3: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(4)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    mapStore(m, k2, v2)
    mapStore(m, k3, v3)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries5<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V, k2: K, v2: V, k3: K, v3: V, k4: K, v4: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(5)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    mapStore(m, k2, v2)
    mapStore(m, k3, v3)
    mapStore(m, k4, v4)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries6<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V, k2: K, v2: V, k3: K, v3: V, k4: K, v4: V, k5: K, v5: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(6)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    mapStore(m, k2, v2)
    mapStore(m, k3, v3)
    mapStore(m, k4, v4)
    mapStore(m, k5, v5)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries7<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V, k2: K, v2: V, k3: K, v3: V, k4: K, v4: V, k5: K, v5: V, k6: K, v6: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(7)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    mapStore(m, k2, v2)
    mapStore(m, k3, v3)
    mapStore(m, k4, v4)
    mapStore(m, k5, v5)
    mapStore(m, k6, v6)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries8<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V, k2: K, v2: V, k3: K, v3: V, k4: K, v4: V, k5: K, v5: V, k6: K, v6: V, k7: K, v7: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(8)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    mapStore(m, k2, v2)
    mapStore(m, k3, v3)
    mapStore(m, k4, v4)
    mapStore(m, k5, v5)
    mapStore(m, k6, v6)
    mapStore(m, k7, v7)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries9<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V, k2: K, v2: V, k3: K, v3: V, k4: K, v4: V, k5: K, v5: V, k6: K, v6: V, k7: K, v7: V, k8: K, v8: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(9)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    mapStore(m, k2, v2)
    mapStore(m, k3, v3)
    mapStore(m, k4, v4)
    mapStore(m, k5, v5)
    mapStore(m, k6, v6)
    mapStore(m, k7, v7)
    mapStore(m, k8, v8)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries10<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V, k2: K, v2: V, k3: K, v3: V, k4: K, v4: V, k5: K, v5: V, k6: K, v6: V, k7: K, v7: V, k8: K, v8: V, k9: K, v9: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(10)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    mapStore(m, k2, v2)
    mapStore(m, k3, v3)
    mapStore(m, k4, v4)
    mapStore(m, k5, v5)
    mapStore(m, k6, v6)
    mapStore(m, k7, v7)
    mapStore(m, k8, v8)
    mapStore(m, k9, v9)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries11<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V, k2: K, v2: V, k3: K, v3: V, k4: K, v4: V, k5: K, v5: V, k6: K, v6: V, k7: K, v7: V, k8: K, v8: V, k9: K, v9: V, k10: K, v10: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(11)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    mapStore(m, k2, v2)
    mapStore(m, k3, v3)
    mapStore(m, k4, v4)
    mapStore(m, k5, v5)
    mapStore(m, k6, v6)
    mapStore(m, k7, v7)
    mapStore(m, k8, v8)
    mapStore(m, k9, v9)
    mapStore(m, k10, v10)
    return m
}

public fn mapEntries12<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(k0: K, v0: V, k1: K, v1: V, k2: K, v2: V, k3: K, v3: V, k4: K, v4: V, k5: K, v5: V, k6: K, v6: V, k7: K, v7: V, k8: K, v8: V, k9: K, v9: V, k10: K, v10: V, k11: K, v11: V) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(12)
    mapStore(m, k0, v0)
    mapStore(m, k1, v1)
    mapStore(m, k2, v2)
    mapStore(m, k3, v3)
    mapStore(m, k4, v4)
    mapStore(m, k5, v5)
    mapStore(m, k6, v6)
    mapStore(m, k7, v7)
    mapStore(m, k8, v8)
    mapStore(m, k9, v9)
    mapStore(m, k10, v10)
    mapStore(m, k11, v11)
    return m
}

// A map literal of more than twelve entries with a computed owned key or value: sema builds one
// `mapEntriesN` per run of twelve and merges the runs, so every key and value is still an
// argument of a call that releases it (src/sema/maps.psm). A producer: it builds the map it
// returns, and its two arguments are temporaries released after the call. An entry of
// `second` replaces one of `first` with the same key, as the chain of `set`s this stands for
// would. Copies go through `keyAt` and `valueAt`, so no view of a column element is live
// while the new map is stored into (a view across a store is what keeps a column from
// releasing at once, src/ir/columns.psm).
lang("map_merge") public fn mapMerge<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(first: HashMap<K, V>, second: HashMap<K, V>) -> HashMap<K, V> {
    let m: HashMap<K, V> = mapWithCapacity(list_len(first.entryKeys) + list_len(second.entryKeys))
    for i in 0..<list_len(first.entryKeys) { mapStore(m, first.keyAt(i), first.valueAt(i)) }
    for i in 0..<list_len(second.entryKeys) { mapStore(m, second.keyAt(i), second.valueAt(i)) }
    return m
}

// **Both modes answer a hit with the dense entry index.** They differ only in
// what a miss is: `findSlot` reports the empty bucket it stopped on, encoded as
// `-bucket - 2`, and a lookup reports -1.
//
// Encoding the bucket rather than returning it plainly is what removes the last
// redundant load from `set`. It used to ask for the bucket, get one whether
// the key was there or not, and then re-read `slots[at]` to find out which --
// a load, a bounds check and a representation test for an answer the probe had
// already computed and thrown away. A bucket is in `[0, mask]`, so `-bucket - 2`
// is at most -2 and can never be confused with an entry index or with the
// lookup miss. (`mask` would have to reach 2^31-1 for that subtraction to wrap,
// which is an index table of 8 GB.)
//
// Triangular offsets visit every bucket of a power-of-two table, and growth
// keeps an empty bucket available, so probing terminates. Advancing by
// 1, 2, 3, ... reduces primary clustering while retaining the compact
// insertion-order index table.
// The bucket hash. `hash(key)` is the key type's answer; a map that has caught
// itself clustering re-mixes it, and every map starts out trusting the key.
//
// **The branch is the cheap half of the trade.** It is one load and one
// perfectly-predicted test per probe, against the eight instructions of an
// unconditional avalanche mix — and against the locality that mix destroys,
// which is the larger number: `key_value_update`'s update phase is 7.25 ms with
// the mix applied to every key and 3.64 ms without it.
private fn mapHashOf<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(m: HashMap<K, V>, key: K) -> Int {
    let h = hash(key)
    if (m.strong) { return keyStrengthen(h) }
    return h
}

// `inline(always)`: every lookup and update is this loop, and its callers' loops want it in
// their own bodies (the comment on HashMap says why it needs telling).
private inline(always) fn mapProbe<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(m: HashMap<K, V>, key: K, findSlot: Bool) -> Int {
    let mut at = mapHashOf(m, key) & m.mask
    let mut step = 1
    let mut reusable = -1
    let mut reusableStep = 0
    loop {
        __builtin_assume((at as U32) < (list_len(m.slots) as U32))
        let entry = list_get(m.slots, at)
        if (entry < 0) {
            if (entry == mapEmpty()) {
                if (findSlot) {
                    if (reusable >= 0) {
                        m.placed = reusableStep - 1
                        return 0 - reusable - 2
                    }
                    m.placed = step - 1
                    return 0 - at - 2
                }
                return -1
            }
            // A tombstone: the key may be further along, and a new key may go here.
            if (reusable < 0) {
                reusable = at
                reusableStep = step
            }
        } else {
            __builtin_assume((entry as U32) < (list_len(m.entryKeys) as U32))
            let existing = list_get(m.entryKeys, entry)
            // Trait-qualified: `Eq` spells its method `eq` too, and a program that
            // imports std.vec has both traits in scope.
            if (Key.eq(existing, key)) { return entry }
        }
        // `longest + 1` buckets probed, and no entry sits further from its home:
        // the key is absent. **This is what makes a removal cheap to undo.**
        // Consecutive integer keys under the identity hash fill one unbroken
        // run of buckets, a removed key's home becomes a tombstone, and without
        // this its reinsertion probed to the end of the run before it could
        // reuse it -- mixed_map_removal spent 148 of its 155 ms there. An
        // insertion that has passed a tombstone takes it; one that has not goes
        // on to an empty bucket.
        if (step > m.longest) {
            if (findSlot == false) { return -1 }
            if (reusable >= 0) {
                m.placed = reusableStep - 1
                return 0 - reusable - 2
            }
        }
        at = (at + step) & m.mask
        step = step + 1
    }
}

// The bucket holding entry `entry`, found along its key's probe chain. The
// entry exists, so the chain reaches it.
private fn mapBucketOfEntry<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(m: HashMap<K, V>, entry: Int) -> Int {
    let mut at = mapHashOf(m, list_get(m.entryKeys, entry)) & m.mask
    let mut step = 1
    while (list_get(m.slots, at) != entry) {
        at = (at + step) & m.mask
        step = step + 1
    }
    return at
}

// Doubles the table and re-places every entry.
//
// **The slot list is grown in place rather than replaced**, and that is not a
// micro-optimisation: assigning a new `List` over `m.slots` releases nothing, so
// every rehash would leak the old table. Pushing the new half and then clearing
// the old half reuses the one allocation the map has ever had for this.
private fn mapRehash<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(m: HashMap<K, V>) {
    let old = m.mask + 1
    let capacity = old * 2

    let mut i = old
    while (i < capacity) {
        list_push(m.slots, -1)
        i = i + 1
    }
    m.mask = capacity - 1

    // **The table checks the hash it is being given, and this is the whole
    // defence of a cheap `keyMixInt`.** Placing every entry into a table it has
    // just doubled costs about 0.2 displaced probes per entry when the hashes
    // spread; three per entry is not a run of bad luck at any size, it is a hash
    // that is not separating these keys. `i * 65537` under the fold in
    // std/key.psm is the family that does it — the two halves cancel and every
    // key lands in bucket 0 — and it reaches an average of three by the ninth
    // entry, so the wasted work before the switch is thirty-six probe steps.
    //
    // A map that switches never switches back: the keys that clustered are
    // still in it, and the strengthened hash is what is now keeping them apart.
    // A false positive costs that map its locality and nothing else, which is
    // why the threshold does not need to be delicate.
    if (mapPlaceAll(m) > list_len(m.entryKeys) * 3 and m.strong == false) {
        m.strong = true
        mapPlaceAll(m)
    }
}

// Clear the index table and re-place every entry, answering with the total
// probe displacement — the number of buckets that were already taken.
//
// Existing entries are unique, so this needs an empty bucket and never a key
// comparison; that is work insertion already did.
private fn mapPlaceAll<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(m: HashMap<K, V>) -> Int {
    let capacity = m.mask + 1
    let mut j = 0
    while (j < capacity) {
        list_set(m.slots, j, mapEmpty())
        j = j + 1
    }
    m.tombstones = 0
    m.longest = 0

    let n = list_len(m.entryKeys)
    let mut displacement = 0
    let mut e = 0
    while (e < n) {
        let key = list_get(m.entryKeys, e)
        let mut at = mapHashOf(m, key) & m.mask
        let mut step = 1
        while (list_get(m.slots, at) >= 0) {
            at = (at + step) & m.mask
            step = step + 1
            displacement = displacement + 1
        }
        list_set(m.slots, at, e)
        if (step - 1 > m.longest) { m.longest = step - 1 }
        e = e + 1
    }
    return displacement
}

// The half of `set` that runs when the key is new, kept in its own function
// so that the half that runs when it is not can be inlined into a caller's loop.
// Grows above 1/2 load after insertion. A spare index bucket costs four bytes;
// keeping probe chains short is worth that space on lookup/update workloads.
// Keys and values remain dense, so lowering occupancy only grows the index.
// Comparing with capacity / 2 also avoids overflowing count * 4 at large sizes.
//
// **This is a codegen shape, not a refactor, and it is worth 1.5x on an update
// loop.** `set` overwriting an existing key is a probe and one store; the
// insert path is two pushes, a copy, a slot write and a possible rehash. Inlined
// together they are one body far over any inliner's threshold, so
// `m.set(k, m.getOr(k, 0) + 1)` compiled to two out-of-line calls that
// hash the same key twice and probe the same bucket twice, with nothing able to
// see that they were the same key. Split, the hot half inlines and both probes
// land in the caller where the optimiser can look at them together.
//
// **`cold`, because the split is not enough on its own.** An executable
// internalises its functions, and LLVM inlines an internal function's only call
// however large it is: `mapInsert` went straight back into `set`, which then
// inlined nowhere, and key_value_update measured 1.28x until this was marked.
private cold fn mapInsert<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(m: HashMap<K, V>, key: K, value: V, at: Int) {
    // The key is **copied in**, not moved: a parameter is a borrow, so
    // `list_push(m.entryKeys, key)` is "cannot move out of borrowed value", which is
    // exactly why an owned key needs the separate `Copy` bound. A
    // `sink` parameter pushed here would be worse than either -- codegen
    // transfers nothing, so the caller's scope exit and the list's release both
    // free it, and `--verify` reports "release of a pointer that is not live".
    //
    // The **value** is not copied, and that is the limitation below.
    let index = list_len(m.entryKeys)
    list_push(m.entryKeys, copyOf(key))
    list_push(m.entryValues, copyOf(value))
    if (list_get(m.slots, at) == mapTombstone()) { m.tombstones = m.tombstones - 1 }
    list_set(m.slots, at, index)
    if (m.placed > m.longest) { m.longest = m.placed }

    if ((index + 1 + m.tombstones) > (m.mask + 1) / 2) { mapRehash(m) }
}

// Removes the entry at dense position `entry`. The last entry moves into its
// position, so positions from `keyAt` are insertion order only until the first
// removal; see the header. The removed key is released by the truncate, having
// been swapped to the end: overwriting it with `list_set` would not release it.
private fn mapRemoveEntry<K: Key + Copy, V: Copy>(m: HashMap<K, V>, entry: Int) {
    let bucket = mapBucketOfEntry(m, entry)
    let last = list_len(m.entryKeys) - 1
    if (entry != last) {
        list_set(m.slots, mapBucketOfEntry(m, last), entry)
        list_swap(m.entryKeys, entry, last)
        list_swap(m.entryValues, entry, last)
    }
    list_set(m.slots, bucket, mapTombstone())
    list_truncate(m.entryKeys, last)
    list_truncate(m.entryValues, last)
    m.tombstones = m.tombstones + 1
    if (m.tombstones > (m.mask + 1) / 4) { mapPlaceAll(m) }
}

// The methods are the API; the `map*` functions above are what the compiler's
// literal and `for` lowerings call.
//
//     let ages = HashMap<String, Int>()      // or `let ages: HashMap<String, Int>()`
//     ages["ada"] = 36                        // insert or replace
//     ages.set("grace", 45)                   // the same, answering the previous value
//     if (ages.contains("ada")) { println(ages["ada"]) }
//     ages.remove("ada")
//
//     let stock: HashMap<String, Int> = { "apple": 3, "pear": 5 }
//     let empty: HashMap<String, Int>        // an empty HashMap, as `= {}` is
//
// **Reading and writing have different failure semantics, on purpose.** `m[key]`
// reads and panics when the key is absent; `m[key] = value` inserts or replaces;
// `m.get(key)` answers an optional. `insert` and `set` are the two ways to store
// with an answer: `insert` stores only a new key and says whether it was new (as
// `HashSet.insert` does), `set` stores unconditionally and hands back the value it
// displaced.
impl < K: Key + Copy, V: Copy > HashMap<K, V> {
    // `HashMap<K, V>()` is this (src/sema/checker.psm, semaTypeCall).
    public fn new() -> HashMap<K, V> { return mapNew<K, V>() }

    // Room for `count` entries before the table first grows.
    public fn withCapacity(count: Int) -> HashMap<K, V> { return mapWithCapacity<K, V>(count) }

    // The number of entries.
    public prop count(self) -> Int { return list_len(self.entryKeys) }
    // Whether there are no entries.
    public prop isEmpty(self) -> Bool { return list_len(self.entryKeys) == 0 }
    // How many entries the map holds before the table next grows: what `withCapacity`
    // and `reserve` set, and what `clear` keeps.
    public prop capacity(self) -> Int { return (self.mask + 1) / 2 }

    // Whether the map has an entry for `key`.
    public fn contains(self, key: K) -> Bool { return mapProbe(self, key, false) >= 0 }

    // The value for `key`, or `none`.
    //
    // This is the lookup that can distinguish "absent" from "present and equal to
    // the fallback", which getOr cannot.
    public fn get(self, key: K) -> V? {
        let at = mapProbe(self, key, false)
        if (at < 0) { return none }
        return copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, at))
    }

    // The value for `key`, or `fallback`. For the many callers that have a
    // sensible default and do not want to match on an optional.
    public fn getOr(self, key: K, fallback: V) -> V {
        let at = mapProbe(self, key, false)
        if (at < 0) { return copyOf(fallback) }
        __builtin_assume((at as U32) < (list_len(self.entryValues) as U32))
        return copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, at))
    }

    // `m[key]`: the value, or a panic when there is none. `get` is the spelling
    // for a key that may be absent.
    public fn at(self, key: K) -> V {
        let found = mapProbe(self, key, false)
        if (found < 0) { panic("key not in map") }
        return copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, found))
    }

    // Stores `value` under `key` unless the key is already there, with one
    // lookup; true when it was new. An existing value is left alone.
    public fn insert(self, key: K, value: V) -> Bool {
        let found = mapProbe(self, key, true)
        if (found >= 0) { return false }
        mapInsert(self, key, value, 0 - found - 2)
        return true
    }

    // Stores `value` under `key`, replacing what was there, and answers the value
    // it replaced -- `none` when the key is new. `m[key] = value` is this with the
    // answer dropped.
    public inline(always) fn set(self, key: K, value: V) -> V? {
        let found = mapProbe(self, key, true)
        if (found >= 0) {
            __builtin_assume((found as U32) < (list_len(self.entryValues) as U32))
            let previous = copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, found))
            list_set(self.entryValues, found, copyOf(value))
            return previous
        }
        mapInsert(self, key, value, 0 - found - 2)
        return none
    }

    // The value for `key`, storing `value` first when the key is absent: one
    // lookup where `if not m.contains(k) { m.set(k, v) }` then a read is three.
    public fn getOrInsert(self, key: K, value: V) -> V {
        let found = mapProbe(self, key, true)
        if (found >= 0) { return copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, found)) }
        mapInsert(self, key, value, 0 - found - 2)
        return copyOf(value)
    }

    // `getOrInsert` with the value built only when the key is absent:
    // `m.getOrInsertWith(k, || expensive())`. **`make` runs at most once and must
    // not touch this map.** The key is probed again after it returns, so a `make`
    // that did store this key is not overwritten -- that value is the answer.
    public fn getOrInsertWith<F>(self, key: K, make: F) -> V {
        let found = mapProbe(self, key, true)
        if (found >= 0) { return copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, found)) }
        let value = make.call()
        let again = mapProbe(self, key, true)
        if (again >= 0) { return copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, again)) }
        mapInsert(self, key, value, 0 - again - 2)
        return copyOf(value)
    }

    // Replaces an existing value with one key lookup. The callback receives the old
    // value and returns its replacement; it runs exactly once on a hit and never
    // on a miss. Returns whether the key existed. Does not insert or reorder.
    // Example: counts.update(key, |value: Int| value + 1).
    public fn update<F>(self, key: K, update: F) -> Bool {
        let at = mapProbe(self, key, false)
        if (at < 0) { return false }
        let value = update.call(copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, at)))
        list_set(self.entryValues, at, copyOf(value))
        return true
    }

    // `update` that also stores: replaces the value under `key` with `change(old)`, or
    // inserts `initial` when the key is absent, with one lookup. The counting idiom:
    // `counts.upsert(word, 1, |n: Int| n + 1)`. Like `update`, `change` runs once, on a
    // hit only, and must not touch this map.
    public fn upsert<F>(self, key: K, initial: V, change: F) {
        let found = mapProbe(self, key, true)
        if (found >= 0) {
            let value = change.call(copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, found)))
            list_set(self.entryValues, found, copyOf(value))
            return
        }
        mapInsert(self, key, initial, 0 - found - 2)
    }

    // Changes the value under `key` where it is stored, and answers whether the key was
    // there. `change` is lent the stored value itself, so a `Vec` or a struct value is
    // changed with no copy and no store back: `m.modify(key, |inout v: Vec<Int>| { v.push(x) })`
    // where `m[key].push(x)` would read a copy, change it and store it, copying the `Vec`
    // twice. The parameter is `inout`, which a `Vec`, a struct, an array or a slice can
    // be; for an `Int` or a `String` value, `update` is the spelling. `change` runs once, on
    // a hit only, and must not touch this map: a closure cannot capture the map it is
    // lent from (a capture is a move or a copy), and one that reaches it through a
    // global only keeps the column from releasing at the store (src/ir/columns.psm).
    public fn modify<F>(self, key: K, change: F) -> Bool {
        let at = mapProbe(self, key, false)
        if (at < 0) { return false }
        change.call(self.entryValues[at])
        return true
    }

    // Removes `key`, answering whether it was there. The value goes with it, and
    // the last entry moves into its place.
    public fn remove(self, key: K) -> Bool {
        let entry = mapProbe(self, key, false)
        if (entry < 0) { return false }
        mapRemoveEntry(self, entry)
        return true
    }

    // Removes `key` and answers the value it held, or `none`. `remove` is this
    // for a caller that does not want the value.
    public fn take(self, key: K) -> V? {
        let entry = mapProbe(self, key, false)
        if (entry < 0) { return none }
        let value = copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, entry))
        mapRemoveEntry(self, entry)
        return value
    }

    // Keeps the entries `keep(key, value)` accepts and removes the rest, in one
    // pass. **The kept entries keep their relative order** (unlike `remove`): they
    // are swapped forward and the rest truncated off the end, as Vec.retain does,
    // and the probe table is rebuilt once.
    // Example: m.retain(|key: String, value: Int| value > 10).
    public fn retain<F>(self, keep: F) {
        let n = list_len(self.entryKeys)
        let mut write = 0
        for read in 0..<n {
            if (keep.call(copyOf(list_get(self.entryKeys, read)), copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, read)))) {
                if (read != write) {
                    list_swap(self.entryKeys, write, read)
                    list_swap(self.entryValues, write, read)
                }
                write = write + 1
            }
        }
        if (write == n) { return }
        list_truncate(self.entryKeys, write)
        list_truncate(self.entryValues, write)
        mapPlaceAll(self)
    }

    // Room for `additional` more entries on top of what the map holds, so the
    // inserts that follow do not grow the table. (`withCapacity(n)` is the
    // initial size of an empty map; this is relative, as Rust's `reserve` is.)
    public fn reserve(self, additional: Int) {
        let want = list_len(self.entryKeys) + additional
        let mut buckets = self.mask + 1
        if (buckets / 2 >= want) { return }
        while (buckets / 2 < want) { buckets = buckets * 2 }
        list_reserve(self.entryKeys, want)
        list_reserve(self.entryValues, want)
        list_reserve(self.slots, buckets)
        for i in (self.mask + 1)..<buckets { list_push(self.slots, -1) }
        self.mask = buckets - 1
        mapPlaceAll(self)
    }

    // Gives back the room past what the map holds: the columns shrink to their length and the
    // probe table to the smallest power of two that keeps the entries at or under half full
    // (never fewer buckets than a new map's), which also clears the tombstones. `capacity`
    // reads what is left. The next insert past it grows again.
    public fn shrinkToFit(self) {
        let want = list_len(self.entryKeys)
        let mut buckets = mapInitialCapacity()
        while (buckets / 2 < want) { buckets = buckets * 2 }
        list_shrink_to_fit(self.entryKeys)
        list_shrink_to_fit(self.entryValues)
        if (buckets < self.mask + 1) {
            list_truncate(self.slots, buckets)
            list_shrink_to_fit(self.slots)
            self.mask = buckets - 1
        }
        mapPlaceAll(self)
    }

    // Removes every entry, releasing the keys and values, and **keeps the table's
    // capacity**: a map cleared and refilled to its old size does not grow again.
    public fn clear(self) {
        list_truncate(self.entryKeys, 0)
        list_truncate(self.entryValues, 0)
        mapPlaceAll(self)
    }

    // The keys, in position order, as the `Vec` `values()` is for the values. The
    // `Vec` owns its own copy of each key and releases it with the rest; the map
    // keeps its own. `keyAt` reads one in place when no copy is wanted.
    public fn keys(self) -> Vec<K> {
        let mut out: Vec<K> = []
        for i in 0..<list_len(self.entryKeys) { out.push(copyOf(list_get(self.entryKeys, i))) }
        return out
    }

    // The values, in position order. A value is never owned (see the header),
    // so this copies nothing that needs releasing.
    public fn values(self) -> Vec<V> {
        let mut out: Vec<V> = []
        for i in 0..<list_len(self.entryValues) { out.push(copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, i))) }
        return out
    }

    // The key and value at a position in `[0, count)`, for iterating. Positions are
    // insertion order, and stay valid until the next insert or removal.
    public fn keyAt(self, index: Int) -> K { return copyOf(list_get(self.entryKeys, index)) }
    // The value of the entry at position `index` in insertion order.
    public fn valueAt(self, index: Int) -> V { return copyOf(list_get(self.entryValues, index)) }
}

// HashSet<K> -- a collection of distinct keys.
//
//     let seen = HashSet<String>()
//     seen.insert("ada")             // true: it was not there
//     seen.insert("ada")             // false: it was
//     if (seen.contains("ada")) { println("known") }
//     seen.remove("ada")             // true: it was there
//
// **The table above with the value column taken out, and the probe written out
// again rather than shared.** The first version was a `HashMap<K, Bool>` in one field,
// and it leaked its whole table: a struct holding another struct that owns columns is
// not released through the field (a `HashMap` in a field of a user struct leaks the
// same way, KNOWN_ISSUES "A pass-through result in a struct field is never released"),
// and `HashSet<Int>` read 7 allocated, 1 released. A set that holds its own columns is
// released like a map is.
//
// Everything about the representation is HashMap's -- an insertion-ordered dense
// `entryKeys`, an open-addressed `slots` of indices into it, triangular probing,
// tombstones, swap-removal, the `longest` displacement bound, the `strong` re-mix --
// and so is every `__builtin_assume`: `slots` has `mask + 1` elements, and a bucket's
// entry index is below `entryKeys`'s length. Keys are `K: Key + Copy`, copied in on
// insertion and released with the table. Changing the probe in one table is a
// change to the other; tests/test_369_hash_set.psm runs both through the same shapes.
lang("hash_set") public struct HashSet<K: Key + Copy> {
    entryKeys: Vec<K>,
    slots: Vec<Int>,
    mask: Int,
    tombstones: Int,
    longest: Int,
    placed: Int,
    strong: Bool
}

// The one place a set is built, as `mapNew` is for a map, so that each column has one
// producer. `HashSet<K>()`, `withCapacity` and `let s: HashSet<K>`
// (src/sema/defaults.psm) all come here.
lang("hash_set_new") public fn hashSetNew<K: Key + Copy>() -> HashSet<K> {
    let mut k: Vec<K> = list_new()
    let mut s: Vec<Int> = list_new_with_capacity(mapInitialCapacity())
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < mapInitialCapacity()) {
        list_push(s, -1)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return HashSet<K> { entryKeys: k, slots: s, mask: mapInitialCapacity() - 1,
                        tombstones: 0, longest: 0, placed: 0, strong: false }
}

private fn hashSetHashOf<K: Key + Copy>(set: HashSet<K>, key: K) -> Int {
    let h = hash(key)
    if (set.strong) { return keyStrengthen(h) }
    return h
}

// mapProbe, for a set: the dense index of `key`, or -1 when `findSlot` is false and
// the key is absent; with `findSlot` an absent key answers `-bucket - 2`, the bucket it
// belongs in. See mapProbe for why the answer is encoded.
private inline(always) fn hashSetProbe<K: Key + Copy>(set: HashSet<K>, key: K, findSlot: Bool) -> Int {
    let mut at = hashSetHashOf(set, key) & set.mask
    let mut step = 1
    let mut reusable = -1
    let mut reusableStep = 0
    loop {
        __builtin_assume((at as U32) < (list_len(set.slots) as U32))
        let entry = list_get(set.slots, at)
        if (entry < 0) {
            if (entry == mapEmpty()) {
                if (findSlot) {
                    if (reusable >= 0) {
                        set.placed = reusableStep - 1
                        return 0 - reusable - 2
                    }
                    set.placed = step - 1
                    return 0 - at - 2
                }
                return -1
            }
            if (reusable < 0) {
                reusable = at
                reusableStep = step
            }
        } else {
            __builtin_assume((entry as U32) < (list_len(set.entryKeys) as U32))
            let existing = list_get(set.entryKeys, entry)
            if (Key.eq(existing, key)) { return entry }
        }
        if (step > set.longest) {
            if (findSlot == false) { return -1 }
            if (reusable >= 0) {
                set.placed = reusableStep - 1
                return 0 - reusable - 2
            }
        }
        at = (at + step) & set.mask
        step = step + 1
    }
}

private fn hashSetBucketOfEntry<K: Key + Copy>(set: HashSet<K>, entry: Int) -> Int {
    let mut at = hashSetHashOf(set, list_get(set.entryKeys, entry)) & set.mask
    let mut step = 1
    while (list_get(set.slots, at) != entry) {
        at = (at + step) & set.mask
        step = step + 1
    }
    return at
}

// Doubles the table and re-places every key, growing `slots` in place for the
// reason mapRehash does.
private fn hashSetRehash<K: Key + Copy>(set: HashSet<K>) {
    let old = set.mask + 1
    let capacity = old * 2
    let mut i = old
    while (i < capacity) {
        list_push(set.slots, -1)
        i = i + 1
    }
    set.mask = capacity - 1
    if (hashSetPlaceAll(set) > list_len(set.entryKeys) * 3 and set.strong == false) {
        set.strong = true
        hashSetPlaceAll(set)
    }
}

// Clears the index table and re-places every key, answering with the total probe
// displacement.
private fn hashSetPlaceAll<K: Key + Copy>(set: HashSet<K>) -> Int {
    let capacity = set.mask + 1
    let mut j = 0
    while (j < capacity) {
        list_set(set.slots, j, mapEmpty())
        j = j + 1
    }
    set.tombstones = 0
    set.longest = 0

    let n = list_len(set.entryKeys)
    let mut displacement = 0
    let mut e = 0
    while (e < n) {
        let key = list_get(set.entryKeys, e)
        let mut at = hashSetHashOf(set, key) & set.mask
        let mut step = 1
        while (list_get(set.slots, at) >= 0) {
            at = (at + step) & set.mask
            step = step + 1
            displacement = displacement + 1
        }
        list_set(set.slots, at, e)
        if (step - 1 > set.longest) { set.longest = step - 1 }
        e = e + 1
    }
    return displacement
}

// The half of `insert` that runs when the key is new; `cold` for mapInsert's reason.
private cold fn hashSetInsert<K: Key + Copy>(set: HashSet<K>, key: K, at: Int) {
    let index = list_len(set.entryKeys)
    list_push(set.entryKeys, copyOf(key))
    if (list_get(set.slots, at) == mapTombstone()) { set.tombstones = set.tombstones - 1 }
    list_set(set.slots, at, index)
    if (set.placed > set.longest) { set.longest = set.placed }
    if ((index + 1 + set.tombstones) > (set.mask + 1) / 2) { hashSetRehash(set) }
}

impl < K: Key + Copy > HashSet<K> {
    // `HashSet<K>()` is this.
    public fn new() -> HashSet<K> { return hashSetNew<K>() }

    // Room for `count` keys before the table first grows. Grown from `hashSetNew`,
    // not built beside it, for mapWithCapacity's reason.
    public fn withCapacity(count: Int) -> HashSet<K> {
        let set: HashSet<K> = hashSetNew<K>()
        let mut buckets = set.mask + 1
        if (buckets / 2 >= count) { return set }
        while (buckets / 2 < count) { buckets = buckets * 2 }
        list_reserve(set.entryKeys, count)
        list_reserve(set.slots, buckets)
        for i in (set.mask + 1)..<buckets { list_push(set.slots, -1) }
        set.mask = buckets - 1
        return set
    }

    // The number of keys.
    public prop count(self) -> Int { return list_len(self.entryKeys) }
    // Whether there are no keys.
    public prop isEmpty(self) -> Bool { return list_len(self.entryKeys) == 0 }
    // How many keys the set holds before the table next grows: what `withCapacity`
    // sets and `clear` keeps.
    public prop capacity(self) -> Int { return (self.mask + 1) / 2 }

    // Adds `key`; true when it was not there already.
    public fn insert(self, key: K) -> Bool {
        let found = hashSetProbe(self, key, true)
        if (found >= 0) { return false }
        hashSetInsert(self, key, 0 - found - 2)
        return true
    }

    // Whether `key` is in the set.
    public fn contains(self, key: K) -> Bool { return hashSetProbe(self, key, false) >= 0 }

    // Removes `key`, answering whether it was there. The last key moves into its place.
    public fn remove(self, key: K) -> Bool {
        let entry = hashSetProbe(self, key, false)
        if (entry < 0) { return false }
        hashSetRemoveEntry(self, entry)
        return true
    }

    // The key the set stores that equals `key`, or `none`. **A copy**: the set keeps its
    // own, and a `Key + Copy` set cannot hand out the stored one. That is still the
    // answer `contains` cannot give when equal keys differ in what `eq` ignores -- a
    // struct keyed on `id` with a name beside it -- and it is how a canonical value is
    // recovered. Sharing one object between holders (interning) is not
    // something a set can offer: it is shared ownership, which the language does not have
    // (developer docs, performance decisions: "A `Hasher` costs nothing").
    public fn get(self, key: K) -> K? {
        let entry = hashSetProbe(self, key, false)
        if (entry < 0) { return none }
        return copyOf(list_get(self.entryKeys, entry))
    }

    // Removes `key` and answers the key that was stored, or `none`. `remove` for a
    // caller that wants the key back; like `get`, what comes back is a copy, and the
    // stored one is released.
    public fn take(self, key: K) -> K? {
        let entry = hashSetProbe(self, key, false)
        if (entry < 0) { return none }
        let taken = copyOf(list_get(self.entryKeys, entry))
        hashSetRemoveEntry(self, entry)
        return taken
    }

    // Flips membership with one lookup: adds an absent key, removes a present one.
    // Answers whether `key` is in the set afterwards.
    public fn toggle(self, key: K) -> Bool {
        let found = hashSetProbe(self, key, true)
        if (found >= 0) {
            hashSetRemoveEntry(self, found)
            return false
        }
        hashSetInsert(self, key, 0 - found - 2)
        return true
    }

    // Keeps the keys `keep` accepts and removes the rest. **The kept keys keep their
    // relative order.** `keep` is asked about every key before anything is removed, so
    // it may read this set but its answer never depends on the removals.
    // Example: s.retain(|k: Int| k % 2 == 0).
    public fn retain<F>(self, keep: F) {
        let mut flags: Vec<Bool> = []
        for i in 0..<list_len(self.entryKeys) { flags.push(keep.call(copyOf(list_get(self.entryKeys, i)))) }
        hashSetKeepFlagged(self, flags)
    }

    // Room for `additional` more keys on top of what the set holds, so the inserts
    // after it do not grow the table. Relative, as `HashMap.reserve` is.
    public fn reserve(self, additional: Int) {
        let want = list_len(self.entryKeys) + additional
        let mut buckets = self.mask + 1
        if (buckets / 2 >= want) { return }
        while (buckets / 2 < want) { buckets = buckets * 2 }
        list_reserve(self.entryKeys, want)
        list_reserve(self.slots, buckets)
        for i in (self.mask + 1)..<buckets { list_push(self.slots, -1) }
        self.mask = buckets - 1
        hashSetPlaceAll(self)
    }

    // Gives back the room past what the set holds, as `HashMap.shrinkToFit` does.
    public fn shrinkToFit(self) {
        let want = list_len(self.entryKeys)
        let mut buckets = mapInitialCapacity()
        while (buckets / 2 < want) { buckets = buckets * 2 }
        list_shrink_to_fit(self.entryKeys)
        if (buckets < self.mask + 1) {
            list_truncate(self.slots, buckets)
            list_shrink_to_fit(self.slots)
            self.mask = buckets - 1
        }
        hashSetPlaceAll(self)
    }

    // Removes every key, releasing them, and **keeps the table's capacity**.
    public fn clear(self) {
        list_truncate(self.entryKeys, 0)
        hashSetPlaceAll(self)
    }

    // ---- Set algebra. Each answers a new set and leaves both operands alone; the
    // keys of the result are copies. The order of the result is this set's keys, then
    // the other's new ones, and is not a promise.

    // The keys in either set: A ∪ B.
    public fn union(self, other: HashSet<K>) -> HashSet<K> {
        let out: HashSet<K> = hashSetNew<K>()
        out.insertAll(self)
        out.insertAll(other)
        return out
    }

    // The keys in both sets: A ∩ B.
    public fn intersection(self, other: HashSet<K>) -> HashSet<K> {
        let out: HashSet<K> = hashSetNew<K>()
        for i in 0..<list_len(self.entryKeys) {
            let key = copyOf(list_get(self.entryKeys, i))
            if (hashSetProbe(other, key, false) >= 0) { out.insert(key) }
        }
        return out
    }

    // The keys in this set and not in `other`: A − B.
    public fn difference(self, other: HashSet<K>) -> HashSet<K> {
        let out: HashSet<K> = hashSetNew<K>()
        for i in 0..<list_len(self.entryKeys) {
            let key = copyOf(list_get(self.entryKeys, i))
            if (hashSetProbe(other, key, false) < 0) { out.insert(key) }
        }
        return out
    }

    // The keys in exactly one of the two sets: A △ B.
    public fn symmetricDifference(self, other: HashSet<K>) -> HashSet<K> {
        let out: HashSet<K> = hashSetNew<K>()
        for i in 0..<list_len(self.entryKeys) {
            let key = copyOf(list_get(self.entryKeys, i))
            if (hashSetProbe(other, key, false) < 0) { out.insert(key) }
        }
        for i in 0..<list_len(other.entryKeys) {
            let key = copyOf(list_get(other.entryKeys, i))
            if (hashSetProbe(self, key, false) < 0) { out.insert(key) }
        }
        return out
    }

    // Whether every key of this set is in `other`: A ⊆ B.
    public fn isSubsetOf(self, other: HashSet<K>) -> Bool {
        if (list_len(self.entryKeys) > list_len(other.entryKeys)) { return false }
        for i in 0..<list_len(self.entryKeys) {
            if (hashSetProbe(other, list_get(self.entryKeys, i), false) < 0) { return false }
        }
        return true
    }

    // Whether every key of `other` is in this set: A ⊇ B.
    public fn isSupersetOf(self, other: HashSet<K>) -> Bool { return other.isSubsetOf(self) }

    // Whether the two sets share no key.
    public fn isDisjoint(self, other: HashSet<K>) -> Bool {
        for i in 0..<list_len(self.entryKeys) {
            if (hashSetProbe(other, list_get(self.entryKeys, i), false) >= 0) { return false }
        }
        return true
    }

    // ---- Bulk mutation. `other` is a set or a `Vec` of keys.

    // Adds every key of `other`.
    public fn insertAll(self, other: HashSet<K>) {
        for i in 0..<list_len(other.entryKeys) { self.insert(copyOf(list_get(other.entryKeys, i))) }
    }
    public fn insertAll(self, other: Vec<K>) {
        for i in 0..<list_len(other) { self.insert(list_get(other, i)) }
    }

    // Removes every key found in `other`. Walks `other` from the back, so that
    // `s.removeAll(s)` is the removal it reads as: a removal moves the last key into
    // the gap, and the back is the part already done.
    public fn removeAll(self, other: HashSet<K>) {
        let mut i = list_len(other.entryKeys)
        while (i > 0) {
            i = i - 1
            self.remove(copyOf(list_get(other.entryKeys, i)))
        }
    }
    public fn removeAll(self, other: Vec<K>) {
        for i in 0..<list_len(other) { self.remove(list_get(other, i)) }
    }

    // Keeps only the keys found in `other`, in their order. Every key is asked about
    // before any is removed, so `s.retainAll(s)` changes nothing.
    public fn retainAll(self, other: HashSet<K>) {
        let mut flags: Vec<Bool> = []
        for i in 0..<list_len(self.entryKeys) {
            flags.push(hashSetProbe(other, list_get(self.entryKeys, i), false) >= 0)
        }
        hashSetKeepFlagged(self, flags)
    }
    public fn retainAll(self, other: Vec<K>) {
        let lookup: HashSet<K> = hashSetNew<K>()
        lookup.insertAll(other)
        self.retainAll(lookup)
    }
}

// Removes the key at dense position `entry`, moving the last key into it. The
// removed key is released by the truncate, having been swapped to the end.
private fn hashSetRemoveEntry<K: Key + Copy>(set: HashSet<K>, entry: Int) {
    let bucket = hashSetBucketOfEntry(set, entry)
    let last = list_len(set.entryKeys) - 1
    if (entry != last) {
        list_set(set.slots, hashSetBucketOfEntry(set, last), entry)
        list_swap(set.entryKeys, entry, last)
    }
    list_set(set.slots, bucket, mapTombstone())
    list_truncate(set.entryKeys, last)
    set.tombstones = set.tombstones + 1
    if (set.tombstones > (set.mask + 1) / 4) { hashSetPlaceAll(set) }
}

// Keeps the keys whose flag is true, in their order: they are swapped forward and
// the rest truncated off the end, as Vec.retain does, and the probe table is
// rebuilt once.
private fn hashSetKeepFlagged<K: Key + Copy>(set: HashSet<K>, keep: Vec<Bool>) {
    let n = list_len(set.entryKeys)
    let mut write = 0
    for read in 0..<n {
        if (list_get(keep, read)) {
            if (read != write) { list_swap(set.entryKeys, write, read) }
            write = write + 1
        }
    }
    if (write == n) { return }
    list_truncate(set.entryKeys, write)
    hashSetPlaceAll(set)
}

// Not API: the target of `for key in set` (src/sema/checker.psm, semaForEachSet).
// A set has no positional contract, so there is no method for it.
lang("hash_set_key_at") public fn hashSetKeyAt<K: Key + Copy>(set: HashSet<K>, index: Int) -> K {
    return copyOf(list_get(set.entryKeys, index))
}
